                <pb facs="1229" n="1219" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1219"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5034 Registration/6–1945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-19T19:00:00-04:00">June 19, 1945—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>944. Department has received a letter from Soong, the substance of which
                    follows:</p>
                <p>[Here follows text as printed above.]</p>
                <p>You will note divergence between above letter and Soong’s statement to Clayton
                    reported in Department’s 904 of June 14, 4 p.m. It would be to our advantage to
                    proceed upon basis of Soong’s oral statement. Please discuss matter with
                    appropriate authorities, with Soong if possible, with a view to reaching
                    satisfactory understanding of action to be taken by Chinese Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
